# Sprint Backlog

**Project:** Tech Empower 

## Evidence

- 🗂️ [Tech Empower Trello board](https://trello.com/b/NqVLW18P/tech-empower3)

![Trello board showing the Sprint 1, Sprint 2 and Sprint 3 columns](files/product-backlog_png.PNG)

*Figure 1: The Sprint 1, 2 and 3 columns. Each sprint holds daily cards with a 5/5 checklist, and Sprint 1 Day 1 shows its dates (14 Jul - 15 Jul).*

## What the Sprint Backlog is

The actionable plan for one sprint: the specific cards the team pulled from the
Product Backlog to reach the sprint goal. On our board each sprint is a column,
and each day of the sprint is a card with a checklist and all five members
assigned.

## Our sprints

**Sprint 1: Form the team** (Day 1 scheduled 14 Jul to 15 Jul 2026)

| Card | Status |
|---|---|
| Day 1: Form the Project Team | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 2: Assign Team Roles | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 3: Review Assignment Requirements | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 4: Brainstorm Business Ideas | Complete, 5/5 tasks |

**Sprint 2: Choose the business and community**

| Card | Status |
|---|---|
| Day 1: Select Business Idea (Tech Empower Labs) | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 2: Create Trello Board | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 3: Identify Target Community | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 4: Define the Community Problem | Complete, 5/5 tasks |

**Sprint 3: Customer survey**

| Card | Status |
|---|---|
| Day 1: Plan Customer Survey | All 5 tasks ticked |
| Day 2: Create Google Forms Survey | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 3: Share Survey with Learners | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 4: Collect Survey Responses | Not marked complete |

**Sprint 9: Build the website**

| Card | Status |
|---|---|
| Day 1: Design About Us Page | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 2: Develop Service Page | Complete, 5/5 tasks |
| Day 3: Implement Contact Page & Finalize Testing | Complete, 5/5 tasks |.

## My Role

I was the team's Developer / Writer and, with the other four members, I was
assigned to every card in the Sprint 1, 2, 3 and 9 columns. My responsibility on
them was the content and documentation side of the work. In Sprint 9, which built
the website, the written-content tasks (such as drafting the mission statement and
detailing the IT training packages) fit that role.

## Reflection

**What we did:** At the start of each sprint we moved cards from the Product
Backlog into a sprint column, assigned the team and worked through each day's
checklist.

**What I learned:**
- A sprint of three or four daily cards is small enough to finish. Sprints 1 and
  2 are fully complete.
- Each card's five-task checklist told us exactly what was left each day.
- The Sprint 9 cards show the plan turning into a product: the About Us,
  Services and Contact pages for our GitHub Pages website.
- A card with a five-task checklist is a good size: small enough to finish and
  big enough to matter.

**Challenge and what I took from it:** Not every sprint was closed properly.
Sprint 3's last card is not marked complete, and Sprints 4 to 8 were never given
their own columns. The lesson is that a sprint only works as a record if it is
closed at the end.

**How it connects:** Sprint cards come from the Product Backlog, and the Agile
Logbook records what happened on each of them.

**What I would improve:** I would give every sprint its own column and close it
properly. Sprints 4 to 8 were never moved out of the backlog, and Sprint 3's
last card is not marked complete, so the board does not show a full history.

