# User Stories and Tasks

**Project:** Tech Empower | **Subject:** Project Presentation 3 (PRP370S/371S/372S)

## Evidence

- 🗂️ [Tech Empower Trello board](https://trello.com/b/NqVLW18P/tech-empower3)

![Trello card: Sprint 1, Form the Project Team](files/product-backlog-sp1_png.PNG)

*Figure 1: Sprint 1 story card with its description, five-task checklist (100% complete), label and assigned members.*

![Trello card: Sprint 2, Select Business Idea (Tech Empower Labs)](files/product-backlog-sp2_png.PNG)

*Figure 2: Sprint 2 story card. The description holds the user story and the checklist holds its tasks.*

## What a user story is

A short description of a feature from the end user's point of view:
**As a** [user], **I want** [goal], **so that** [benefit]. On our Trello board
each story is written in the card description, and its tasks are the checklist
on the same card.

## Our stories and tasks

**Sprint 1: setting up the team**

| Card | User story | Tasks (checklist) |
|---|---|---|
| Day 1: Form the Project Team | As a project team, we want to organise ourselves so that the project runs smoothly. | Identify group members<br>Exchange contact details<br>Create WhatsApp group<br>Schedule first meeting<br>Confirm final team |
| Day 2: Assign Team Roles | As a project leader, I want to assign roles so that everyone knows their responsibilities. | Assign Project Leader<br>Assign Researcher<br>Assign Designer<br>Assign Presenter<br>Confirm final team |
| Day 3: Review Assignment Requirements | As the Project Leader, I want to review the project requirements so that the team understands the project scope, objectives, and deliverables before starting work. | Read assignment brief<br>Identify marking rubric<br>Record deadlines<br>Clarify unclear requirements<br>Approve project plan |
| Day 4: Brainstorm Business Ideas | As the business owner, I want to brainstorm business ideas so that I can identify the best solution for schools and communities. | Generate business ideas<br>Discuss each idea<br>Identify community problems<br>Record meeting notes<br>Finalise shortlisted idea |

**Sprint 2: choosing the business and the community**

| Card | User story | Tasks (checklist) |
|---|---|---|
| Day 1: Select Business Idea (Tech Empower Labs) | As a school principal, I want the business to offer a practical digital learning solution so that learners can improve their ICT skills. | Present shortlisted ideas<br>Compare advantages<br>Vote as a team<br>Confirm Tech Empower Labs<br>Record decision |
| Day 2: Create Trello Board | As the Project Leader, I want to create a Trello board so that the team can organize, assign, and track project tasks efficiently. | Create Trello workspace<br>Create project board<br>Add team members<br>Create task lists<br>Review board structure |
| Day 3: Identify Target Community | As a community member, I want digital learning services that address my community's needs so that I can improve my IT skills and opportunities. | Identify Cape Town schools<br>Research target schools<br>Identify target customers<br>Record community needs<br>Approve target community |
| Day 4: Define the Community Problem | (no story written on this card) | Describe the problem<br>Identify affected users<br>Collect supporting evidence<br>Review problem statement<br>Approve final problem |

**Sprint 9: building the website**

| Card | User story | Tasks (checklist) |
|---|---|---|
| Day 1: Design About Us Page | As a prospective high school partner or client, I want to view a clear homepage and About Us page detailing Tech Empower's mission, so that I understand who is behind the initiative and our commitment to bridging the digital divide in Cape Town. | Wireframe page layout and hero section<br>Draft mission statement and company vision<br>Add team member profile details<br>Apply CSS styles and colors<br>Review page alignment and mobile view |
| Day 2: Develop Service Page | As a school principal or educator, I want to navigate dedicated Products and Services pages detailing hardware, internet access, and IT training, so that I can easily evaluate how Tech Empower's digital learning services can support our high school students. | Outline hardware offerings (laptops, routers)<br>Detail IT training packages (Word, Excel, Coding)<br>Insert package details<br>Verify navigation links between pages<br>Format service grid/cards |
| Day 3: Implement Contact Page & Finalize Testing | As a site visitor, I want to use an interactive Contact page with a direct form submission, so that I can quickly reach out to book services, request hardware setups, or ask questions. | Build user contact form (Name, Email, Message)<br>Add company contact details and location<br>Test form submission validation<br>Perform cross-browser testing<br>Finalize website code for GitHub Pages deployment |

Cards from Sprint 3 onwards carry the task checklist, but not every card has a
user story written on it.

## My Role

I was the team's Developer / Writer, responsible for content creation,
documentation and technical writing. All five members are assigned to each sprint
card, and my part of the work was the written side. Several tasks in the
checklists above are documentation tasks, such as "Record meeting notes", "Record
deadlines", "Record community needs" and "Record decision", and they fall within
my role of keeping the team's work written down.

## Reflection

**What we did:** We turned the assignment into user stories on Trello cards,
broke each into a checklist of five tasks and worked through them sprint by
sprint, from forming the team in Sprint 1 to building the website in Sprint 9.

**What I learned:**
- A story needs a user and a benefit. Our early Sprint 1 stories are written from
  the team's own point of view ("As a project team", "As the Project Leader"),
  while the later stories name real users: a school principal, a community
  member, a prospective partner and a site visitor. The later ones describe the
  customer's need better.
- Splitting a story into five tasks made the work easy to share and to check.
  Every task has a clear finish, which fits our Definition of Done.
- Verbs make good task names. Tasks such as "Identify", "Record", "Compare" and
  "Approve" are easy to tick off, because it is clear when each one is finished.

**Challenge and what I took from it:** The main challenge was keeping the
stories of five people consistent. Some cards follow the "As a... I want... so
that..." pattern well, while others, such as Sprint 2 Day 4, have no story at all.
The lesson was to check every story against the pattern before moving a card
forward.

**How it connects:** The stories sit on the cards of the Product Backlog,
selected cards form each Sprint Backlog, and progress is recorded in the Agile
Logbook.

**What I would improve:** I would write a user story on every card, because the
Sprint 2 Day 4 card and several later cards only have tasks, and I would write
all stories from the end user's point of view.

