# Product Backlog

**Project:** Tech Empower | **Subject:** Project Presentation 3 (PRP370S/371S/372S)

## Evidence

- 🗂️ [Tech Empower Trello board](https://trello.com/b/NqVLW18P/tech-empower3)

![Trello board with the Product backlog column next to the Sprint 1, 2 and 3 columns](files/product-backlog_png.PNG)

*Figure 1: The Product backlog column (148 cards) beside the sprint columns. Cards are moved right into a sprint column when a sprint starts.*

![Product backlog card: Sprint 1, Form the Project Team](files/product-backlog-sp1_png.PNG)

*Figure 2: A backlog card for Sprint 1 with its user story, label, members and subtasks.*

![Product backlog card: Sprint 2, Select Business Idea (Tech Empower Labs)](files/product-backlog-sp2_png.PNG)

*Figure 3: A backlog card for Sprint 2 with its activity history.*

## What the Product Backlog is

A prioritised list of all the work, features and improvements the product needs.
Ours is the "Product backlog" column on the Trello board. It holds **148 cards**,
ordered from top to bottom in the order the work is planned.

## How our backlog is organised

| Group | Cards | What it contains |
|---|---|---|
| Sprint 1 to 9 cards | 34 | The planned sprints, from "Sprint 1. Form the Project Team" and "Sprint 2. Select Business Idea (Tech Empower Labs)" to "Sprint 9: Create About Us Page" |
| Phase 5 to 14 cards | 114 | Larger pieces of work, each phase with its own cards (see below) |

The phases are: Phase 5 ICT Product Catalogue, Phase 6 Inventory and Suppliers,
Phase 7 Marketing Strategy, Phase 8 Business Feasibility, Phase 9 Computer Lab
Installation, Phase 10 ICT Sales and Customer Support, Phase 11 Business
Operations, Phase 12 Presentation Preparation, Phase 13 Business Growth and
Expansion and Phase 14 Project Closure and Future.

The sprints cover the early work: forming the team, choosing the business,
running the customer survey, analysing the results, defining the business,
planning services and sales, and building the website. The phases that follow
cover the product catalogue, suppliers, marketing, feasibility, computer lab
installation, sales, operations, presentation, growth and closing the project
(for example "Conduct Sprint Retrospective" and "Launch Tech Empower Labs").

## Status of the backlog

- 119 of the 148 cards have a task checklist.
- 17 cards are marked complete and 23 have every checklist item ticked.
- The closing cards (Conduct Sprint Review, Conduct Sprint Retrospective,
  Document Lessons Learned, Officially Close the Project and others) are still
  waiting at the bottom of the list, which is where a backlog keeps work that has
  not started.

## My Role

I was the team's Developer / Writer and, like the rest of the team, I was
assigned to the sprint cards in the backlog (Sprint 1 to Sprint 9). My
responsibility on them was the content and documentation side of the work.

## Reflection

**What we did:** We listed everything the project needs on one board and ordered
it, so that the team could always see what comes next.

**What I learned:**
- One list for all the work meant the whole project was visible at once, from
  the first team meeting to closing the project.
- Grouping cards into sprints and phases showed the order of the work and which
  parts depend on others, such as the survey coming before the business
  definition.
- The Product Backlog and the sprints work together. Only the cards for the
  current sprint are pulled out, so the rest of the 148 cards can wait without
  getting in the way.

**Challenge and what I took from it:** The backlog is large, and with 148 cards
it is hard to see which ones matter next. The sprint columns solve this by
pulling out only a few cards at a time. The lesson is to keep far-off cards rough
and refine them nearer their sprint.

**How it connects:** The backlog is where each user story starts. Cards chosen
for a sprint become the Sprint Backlog, and the Agile Logbook records what
happened as they were completed.

**What I would improve:** I would keep the backlog smaller and more current,
because most of the 114 phase cards are still far from being worked on, and I
would refine them closer to the sprint in which they are needed.

